# Who owns this repository, in the one place GitHub reads it.
#
# A public repository takes pull requests from strangers, which is the point.
# What it must not take is a change that merges without the maintainer having
# looked at it — and "the maintainer looked at it" is a rule GitHub will only
# enforce if a CODEOWNERS entry says who the maintainer is.
#
# This file on its own enforces nothing. It becomes a guardrail when the
# ruleset in .github/rulesets/main.json is active with `require_code_owner_review`
# on, at which point every pull request against the default branch needs a
# review from the owner named below before it can merge. See
# .github/REPOSITORY-HARDENING.md for the settings that turn it on.
#
# The last matching pattern wins, so the catch-all comes first and anything
# needing a narrower owner would come after it.

*                       @aydinmrnv

# The paths where a bad change is worst: CI runs with repository credentials,
# the Action is what other people's workflows execute, and the guardrails in
# .relay/ and src/unattended/ are the reason an unattended run is safe to
# enable at all. Same owner today — listed separately so that stays true on
# purpose rather than by accident if the catch-all is ever widened.
/.github/               @aydinmrnv
/.github/workflows/     @aydinmrnv
/.relay/                @aydinmrnv
/action.yml             @aydinmrnv
/package.json           @aydinmrnv
/src/unattended/        @aydinmrnv
